{
  "file": "src/execution_coverage/progress.rs",
  "item": "mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_right]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This unit-test-only adapter must implement TermLike's horizontal\n movement callback. Indicatif's single-line renderer never calls it;\n the no-op does not participate in application behavior. The PTY\n integration test exercises the actual terminal implementation."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/execution_coverage/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_right]` is on an explicit function with a body, which is a permitted target. Its explanation is fenced citation commentary, so it counts as a tracked justification.\n\nThe explanation is substantive and agrees with the source:\n- `move_cursor_right` is a required method of `indicatif::TermLike`. It sits in the test-only `RecordingTerm` adapter and is a no-op returning `Ok(())`.\n- The sibling no-op callbacks in `RecordingTerm` follow the same pattern. The adapter implements the callbacks the single-line bar draws with (`write_str`, `clear_line`, `width`) with real or recorded behavior.\n- The claim that indicatif's single-line renderer never calls horizontal movement matches my recollection of indicatif's drawing code, which uses `move_cursor_up`, `move_cursor_down`, `clear_line`, `write_line` and `flush`. I did not read the indicatif source here.\n- The PTY claim holds. `tests/authoring_batch.rs` has `run_in_pty` and `run_in_pty_with_args`, which run the real binary on a PTY. Tests such as `terminal_progress_distinguishes_judgments_from_authoring_errors` and `empty_batches_and_early_errors_clear_the_terminal_display` use them to exercise real terminal output.\n\nThe item has no other `{impl}`, `{test}` or `{cite}` reference to the same target, so the citation is not redundant.",
